\section*{B. Binding curves of charged dimers}

We now consider a more realistic scenario, namely, the problem of predicting the binding curves of a dataset of organic molecular dimers that carry an electric charge. We extract 661 different dimers containing \(\mathrm{H}, \mathrm{C}, \mathrm{N}\), and O atoms from the BioFragment Database (BFDb), \({ }^{47}\) where at least one of the two monomers in each dimer configuration has a net charge. This choice ensures that we focus the exercise on a problem for which permanent electrostatic interactions play a prominent role. Contrary to the NaCl toy system, however, one cannot expect that a fixed point-charge model would suffice to predict the binding curves. The dataset contains a multitude of chemical moieties, including neutral polar fragments and highly polarizable groups and provides a realistic assessment of how well a LODE model can perform in practice. For each of the 661 dimers, we consider 13 configurations where the reciprocal distance between the two monomers, defined as the distance between their geometric centers, spans an interval that ranges from a minimum of \(\sim 3 \AA\) to a maximum of \(\sim 8 \AA\). For each of these configurations, unrelaxed binding curves are computed at the density functional theory (DFT/B3LYP) level using the FHI-aims quantumchemistry package. \({ }^{48}\) The training dataset is defined by considering the binding curves of the first 600 dimers out of the total of 661. Predictions are tested on the remaining 61. We also include the isolated monomers in the training set so that the ML model has knowledge of the dissociation limit and compute a few additional reference energies at larger separations, which are, however, not used for training. SOAP and LODE representations are defined within spherical environments of \(r_{\text {cut }}=3.0 \AA\), while the Gaussian width of the density field is chosen to be \(\sigma=0.3\) and \(1.0 \AA\), respectively.

Before carrying out the learning exercise, the reference DFT energies are baselined with respect to the monomer energies so that the model only has to reproduce the interaction energies between the two fragments. Upon this baselining, we find that optimal SOAP performances correspond to a RMSE \(\sim 20 \%\), whereas a suitable combination between SOAP and \(\operatorname{LODE}(v=2)\) allows us to bring the error down to \(\sim 4 \%\). This substantial improvement can be justified by the large discrepancy between the SOAP and SOAP + LODE accuracy in representing the interaction between the monomers at intermediate and large distances. To clarify the issue further, we plot in Fig. 2 the predicted binding curves of 6 test dimers against the reference DFT calculations. We observe that a SOAPbased local description is overall able to capture the short-range interactions with good accuracy. However, it becomes less and less effective as the distance between the monomers increases to the point of being completely blind to changes in interatomic distances when the environments cutoff distance is overcome. Note that the performance of the local model at small separations is degraded substantially by the inclusion of fully dissociated dimers in the training set because the representation cannot distinguish these configurations from those barely beyond the cutoff distance, which correspond to a nonzero value of the binding curve. The SOAP + LODE multiscale description, in contrast, can recognize the changes in separation between the monomers, leading to a smooth asymptotic behavior of the predicted binding curve. Although a linear model incorporating \(\operatorname{LODE}(v=1)\) allows us to halve the error made by SOAP down to \(\sim 10 \%\), it is not sufficiently expressive to achieve predictive accuracy-particularly for binding

